          <child xsi:type="archimate:DiagramObject" id="id-41d892fa70784b1696c09446c3527fd5" targetConnections="id-57a1add830a142b285c023d0816257ae" font="1|Noto Sans|9.0|0|GTK|1|" lineWidth="2" textAlignment="1" fillColor="#99c1f1" alpha="32" archimateElement="id-eb5c39f9b906477a8f18da0ba9a84271">
            <bounds x="864" y="48" width="277" height="361"/>
            <feature name="gradient" value="0"/>
            <feature name="labelExpression" value="${name}&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;class Catalog extends Artifact&#xA;&#xA;  constructor(artifactory)&#xA;&#xA;  render(modelElement, targetElement)&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;"/>
          </child>
          <documentation>The **Catalog** artifact is a powerful engine for dynamically generating tables, lists, or repeating blocks of architectural elements straight from your model. By combining a visual template with a robust querying system, it transforms raw model data into beautifully formatted, presentation-ready overviews.

To configure a Catalog, you create a Group on your canvas and set its **Name** to `Catalog`. The internal structure of this group dictates the layout of your data (typically using Notes to represent column headers and row templates), while inline parameters allow you to precisely query, filter, and sort the output.

### Core Properties

* **Name:** Must begin with `Catalog`. This is also where you define your query and styling parameters (see below).
* **Label Expression:** Sets the heading of the catalog (e.g., printed as an `&lt;h2>` or `&lt;h3>` depending on its nested level in the document).
* **Documentation:** Renders as introductory text directly beneath the heading, right before the actual table or list begins.

### Query &amp; Styling Parameters

You can fine-tune exactly which elements the Catalog retrieves and how they are presented by adding parameters directly into the Name field. You can now use quotes to pass multiple values to a single parameter, such as multiple CSS classes.

*Format example: `Catalog scope=view sort=name class=&quot;catalog-table custom-margin&quot;*`

* **`scope={model | view | object}`**
Determines the search boundary for the catalog's query.
	* `model`: Scans the entire Archi repository.
	* `view`: *(Highly Optimized)* Scans only the visual elements present on the current canvas. This is the recommended scope for large repositories as it significantly improves rendering performance.
	* `object`: Scans for elements that have a specific relationship with a targeted source element.


* **`sort={name | position}`**
Controls the order of the generated items. Use `name` for standard alphabetical sorting, or `position` to intuitively order elements based on their visual X/Y coordinates on your Archi diagram.
* **`pattern=&lt;Regular Expression>`**
Filters the retrieved elements by their name using standard Regex. For example, `pattern=&quot;^SAP.*&quot;` will only include elements whose name starts with &quot;SAP&quot;.
* **`class=&quot;&lt;CSS class name>&quot;`**
Injects one or multiple custom CSS classes into the root HTML container of the catalog. This gives you absolute control over the styling, such as applying specific border colors or avoiding page breaks.
* **`gap=&lt;number>`**
Defines the vertical spacing between repeating blocks. This is especially useful when you are rendering a list of standalone blocks (like User Stories or Requirements) instead of a standard grid table.

> **Visual Template Pro-Tip:**
> To build your layout, simply place visual Notes inside the Catalog group. The ArchiFabric engine interprets the top notes as your table headers, and the notes below them as your repeating row template. Use expression tags like `${name}` or `${property:Lifecycle}` inside the row notes to dynamically inject the data of your queried elements!</documentation>
        </element>
