          <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-974b0437a6fa401b830c2a4eae55bfde" textAlignment="1" alpha="0" borderType="2">
            <bounds x="168" y="60" width="25" height="20"/>
            <sourceConnection id="id-e61367dbe7364d9e95e2c1fb52892387" lineWidth="3" lineColor="#c64600" source="id-974b0437a6fa401b830c2a4eae55bfde" target="id-c78a4cd465d34580b440a9b764a2d2cc" type="1"/>
          </child>
          <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-c78a4cd465d34580b440a9b764a2d2cc" targetConnections="id-e61367dbe7364d9e95e2c1fb52892387" textAlignment="1" alpha="0" borderType="2">
            <bounds x="168" y="432" width="25" height="25"/>
          </child>
          <documentation>The core concept behind ArchiFabric is refreshingly simple: instead of typing out a document in a traditional word processor, you visually &quot;draw&quot; its outline directly within Archi. By creating a dedicated diagram—which we call the **Model Structure**—you can design the flow of your publication just like you would map out an architecture. This visual approach allows you to intuitively organize the chapters, sections, and paragraphs of your upcoming document by simply arranging boxes on a canvas.

Within this *Model Structure*, the building blocks of your document are represented by standard **Visual Group Elements**. In ArchiFabric, these groups function as **Artifacts**, closely aligning with how the TOGAF framework defines them—as specific, reusable views or deliverables that represent aspects of the architecture. Because these Artifacts are modular and independent, you can easily drag, drop, and reuse the same standard chapter templates or catalogs across multiple different document structures, saving you significant time and effort.

Once your visual outline is set, you bring the document to life by connecting these Artifacts to your actual architectural content. You do this by pointing a *targetObject* inside the Artifact to specific views, folders, or elements in your Archi project, which act as the **Target Structure**. As illustrated in the conceptual diagram, when ArchiFabric runs, it processes your *Model Structure* top-to-bottom, retrieves the linked target content, and seamlessly weaves the diagrams and documentation fields together into a beautifully formatted PDF.

A crucial and reassuring aspect of this method is that building a document structure does not alter your underlying Enterprise Architecture model in any way. Because ArchiFabric relies entirely on graphical items (Groups and Notes) to define the layout, they do not create actual ArchiMate concepts or relationships. Your core architecture repository remains completely clean and untouched, while you gain a smart, automated bridge to share your complex data with stakeholders.

While the *Target Structure* provides your architectural data, a complete document also requires narrative text, dynamic references, and specific settings. ArchiFabric handles this by elegantly reusing Archi’s standard features:

* **Narrative Text &amp; Markdown:** You can write your introductions, explanations, and conclusions directly into the **Documentation** field of your visual Artifacts using standard *Markdown* formatting.
* **Dynamic Label Expressions:** ArchiFabric embraces and enriches Archi's standard *Label Expressions*. This powerful feature allows you to use variables (like `${name}`, `${documentation}` or `${property}`) inside your text, ensuring your document automatically updates whenever your underlying model data changes.
* **Configuration Parameters:** You can pass specific configuration settings (such as query boundaries, sorting, or layout choices) directly through the Name field of your visual element. By appending simple parameters behind the artifact's name (for example, Catalog scope=view), you gain precise control over how the engine processes and renders that specific block.
* **Styling &amp; Theming (CSS):** ArchiFabric allows you to fully customize the visual presentation of your final PDF using standard Cascading Style Sheets (CSS). This ensures your generated documents can perfectly match your corporate identity.

**In summary, the ArchiFabric workflow consists of four simple steps:**

1. **Design:** Draw your document outline (*Model Structure*) using visual Artifacts.
2. **Link:** Connect these Artifacts to your architectural data (*Target Structure*).
3. **Enrich:** Add narrative text, dynamic expressions, properties, and styling.
4. **Publish:** Select your outline and run the ArchiFabric script to generate your PDF.</documentation>
        </element>
      </folder>
      <folder name="Markdown" id="id-51189542d68d4641aa30593ba9bc25ff">
        <documentation># Markdown Syntax Test Document

This document is designed to test the rendering of commonly used **Markdown syntax**. It contains headings, paragraphs, text formatting, lists, links, tables, quotations, code blocks, and footnotes.

---

## 1. Headings

Markdown supports multiple heading levels.

### Level 3 Heading

#### Level 4 Heading

##### Level 5 Heading

###### Level 6 Heading

---

## 2. Paragraphs and Text Formatting

This is a normal paragraph containing plain text. A paragraph can contain multiple sentences and should automatically wrap when the available width is reached.

This paragraph demonstrates **bold text**, *italic text*, and ***bold italic text***.

Markdown can also contain ~~strikethrough text~~ when the Markdown implementation supports GitHub Flavored Markdown (GFM).

Inline code can be written using backticks, for example: `console.log(&quot;Hello World&quot;);`.

Special Markdown characters can be escaped using a backslash. For example: *this text should not be italic*.

---

## 3. Unordered Lists

An unordered list can contain multiple levels:

* First item
* Second item
* Third item

  * Nested item
  * Another nested item

    * Third-level item
* Fourth item

---

## 4. Ordered Lists

An ordered list automatically numbers its items:

1. Analyse the current situation
2. Define the desired outcome
3. Design the target architecture
4. Create the transformation roadmap
5. Implement and evaluate the changes

Nested ordered lists can also be used:

1. Architecture

   1. Business Architecture
   2. Application Architecture
   3. Technology Architecture
2. Implementation

   1. Planning
   2. Realisation
   3. Evaluation

---

## 5. Mixed Lists

Lists can combine ordered and unordered structures:

1. Strategic Analysis

   * Goals
   * Drivers
   * Stakeholders
2. Architecture

   * Value Streams
   * Capabilities
   * Resources
3. Transformation

   * Work Packages
   * Deliverables
   * Outcomes

---

## 6. Blockquotes

A simple quotation can be written as follows:

> Architecture is not merely about designing structures; it is about creating coherence between strategy, organisation, information, and technology.

Blockquotes can contain multiple paragraphs:

> Enterprise Architecture provides a coherent view of an organisation.
>
> It helps decision-makers understand dependencies, opportunities, risks, and the consequences of change.

Nested quotations are also possible:

> This is the first level.
>
> > This is a nested quotation.

---

## 7. Links

Markdown supports hyperlinks.

This is a link to [The Open Group](https://www.opengroup.org/).

A URL can also be shown directly:

https://www.opengroup.org/

Links can also be included within normal sentences, such as the documentation for [ArchiMate](https://www.opengroup.org/archimate-forum).

---

## 8. Tables

Tables are commonly supported through GitHub Flavored Markdown.

| Capability              | Description                                                   | Maturity |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | -------: |
| Strategy Development    | Defines the strategic direction of the organisation.          |        4 |
| Enterprise Architecture | Creates coherence between strategy and organisational design. |        3 |
| Portfolio Management    | Prioritises investments and transformation initiatives.       |        3 |
| Information Management  | Ensures information is managed as an organisational resource. |        2 |

Alignment can also be specified:

| Left aligned | Centred | Right aligned |
| :----------- | :-----: | ------------: |
| Value Stream |   High  |           100 |
| Capability   |  Medium |            75 |
| Resource     |   Low   |            25 |

---

## 9. Inline Code

Inline code can be included in normal text.

For example, the JavaScript function `querySelector()` can be used to select an HTML element.

A Markdown parser could be invoked using something similar to `marked.parse(markdown)`.

---

## 10. Code Blocks

A fenced code block without syntax highlighting:

```
This is a plain code block.

Markdown syntax such as **bold** should normally
not be interpreted inside this block.
```

A JavaScript code block:

```javascript
function renderMarkdown(markdown) {
    return marked.parse(markdown);
}

console.log(renderMarkdown(&quot;# Hello World&quot;));
```

A JSON example:

```json
{
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;ArchiFabric&quot;,
    &quot;type&quot;: &quot;Documentation Generator&quot;,
    &quot;markdown&quot;: true
}
```

A shell example:

```bash
npm install marked
npm install marked-footnote
node index.js
```

---

## 11. Horizontal Rules

Horizontal rules can be used to separate sections.

---

The text above should be separated from this paragraph by a horizontal rule.

---

## 12. Special Characters

The following characters are useful for testing character encoding:

**Symbols:** © ® ™ € £ ¥ § ¶ † ‡

**Mathematical:** ± × ÷ ≠ ≤ ≥ ∞

**Arrows:** ← → ↑ ↓ ↔ ⇒

**Quotation marks:** “double quotes” and ‘single quotes’

**Dashes:** hyphen -, en dash – and em dash —

**European characters:** é è ë ï ö ü ä ß ø æ

---

## 13. Footnotes

Footnotes are useful when additional information should be available without interrupting the main text.

Enterprise Architecture provides a structured approach to organisational change.[^1]

ArchiMate is a modelling language for describing enterprise architectures.[^2]

A footnote can contain a longer explanation.[^longnote]

[^1]: Enterprise Architecture connects strategic objectives with the design and transformation of the organisation.

[^2]: ArchiMate is maintained by The Open Group and provides concepts for modelling different aspects of an enterprise architecture.

[^longnote]: This is an example of a longer footnote. A footnote may contain additional context, references, explanations, or other information that would otherwise interrupt the main flow of the document.

---

## 14. Combined Markdown

Markdown elements can be combined in the same section.

### Enterprise Architecture Principles

The following principles guide the architecture:

1. **Business value first**
   Architecture decisions should contribute to measurable organisational value.

2. **Reuse before acquisition**
   Existing capabilities and resources should be evaluated before new solutions are introduced.

3. **Open standards**
   Solutions should preferably use *open and widely adopted standards*.

4. **Information as a resource**
   Information should be managed as a valuable organisational resource.[^information]

> A principle is only useful when it provides meaningful guidance for making decisions.

[^information]: Managing information as a resource requires ownership, governance, quality management, security, and lifecycle management.

---

## 15. Markdown Stress Test

This final paragraph combines **bold**, *italic*, ***bold italic***, ~~strikethrough~~, `inline code`, a [hyperlink](https://www.example.com/), and a footnote.[^final]

> **Important:** A Markdown renderer should process these elements consistently while preserving the semantic structure of the document.

[^final]: This footnote concludes the Markdown syntax test.

---

**End of Markdown Syntax Test Document**
</documentation>
