          <child xsi:type="archimate:DiagramModelReference" id="id-9f33a55db6a84b0ea1691c9fa6b6fa0b" model="id-8746a4633a2d4bdeb04e1df8d9f0c826">
            <bounds x="144" y="180" width="253" height="25"/>
          </child>
          <child xsi:type="archimate:DiagramModelReference" id="id-499280e988b245d49517aadee799f522" model="id-9b9e0572e326469a990d4b86f1b7a8a3">
            <bounds x="144" y="204" width="253" height="25"/>
          </child>
          <child xsi:type="archimate:DiagramModelReference" id="id-e2e02c6e2f2344f0b77e60f088492129" model="id-bec5d1a629594f94b25904ec58faad7f">
            <bounds x="144" y="228" width="253" height="25"/>
          </child>
          <documentation>Now that you are familiar with the basic concepts, it is time to put theory into practice. To make this learning process as smooth as possible, we have prepared a dedicated sample project.

Download the **`tutorial.archimate`** file from the ArchiFabric [GitHub Releases page](https://github.com/Optiprise/archi-fabric/releases) and open it in Archi. In the Models tree on the left, expand the **Views** folder. You will see it is divided into three specific subfolders:

* **Architecture:** This folder contains the actual ArchiMate views we will use as data sources, such as the fully documented `Deliver Customer Value` view.
* **Artifactory:** A storage folder for reusable visual building blocks (Artifacts).
* **Tutorial:** Your empty workspace. We will build our document outlines within this folder.

By following these practical steps, you will experience the workflow firsthand and gain the confidence to build dynamic enterprise documentation.</documentation>
        </child>
      </element>
    </folder>
