        <documentation>Separating the layout from the content is a vital concept in ArchiFabric and a prerequisite for generating automated lists or catalogs later. By simply placing a target view immediately after a template view on your canvas, ArchiFabric automatically applies the template to the target data.

Follow these steps to build your Artifact:

1. In the **Tutorial** folder of your model tree, right-click and create a new ArchiMate View. Name it `View Artifacts`. Open this new canvas.
2. Drag a **Group** onto the canvas. In the Properties panel, set its **Name** to `Section`.
3. **Introduce Dynamic Expressions:** In the Properties panel for this `Section` group, set the **Label Expression** to `${name}`. This introduces ArchiFabric's powerful Expression Engine. When the document is generated, `${name}` will automatically be replaced by the name of the target architecture passed into this template. *(Note: You can even use this engine to dynamically fetch model properties later!)*
4. Inside this `Section`, drag a new **Group** and name it `Documentation`.
5. Inside the same `Section`, drag another **Group** below the Documentation, and name it `Diagram`.
6. **Control Diagram Scaling:** Because we are building an abstract template without a physical target view, we need a way to define the diagram's visual width. Drag a standard **Note** element from the palette and drop it *inside* the `Diagram` group. Resize this Note to your preferred width. ArchiFabric will use the width of this Note as a placeholder to scale the dynamic diagram in your final PDF.
7. *Important:* Do not drag any actual target views into this template. It must remain abstract so it can be reused. This abstract layout represents our *ModelStructure*.
1. In the **Tutorial** folder of your model tree, right-click and create a new ArchiMate View. Name it `View Artifacts`. Open this new canvas.
2. Drag a **Group** onto the canvas. In the Properties panel, set its **Name** to `Section`.
3. **Introduce Dynamic Expressions:** In the Properties panel for this `Section` group, set the **Label Expression** to `${name}`. This introduces ArchiFabric's powerful Expression Engine. When the document is generated, `${name}` will automatically be replaced by the name of the target architecture passed into this template. *(Note: You can even use this engine to dynamically fetch model properties later!)*
4. Inside this `Section`, drag a new **Group** and name it `Documentation`.
5. Inside the same `Section`, drag another **Group** below the Documentation, and name it `Diagram`.
6. **Control Diagram Scaling:** Because we are building an abstract template without a physical target view, we need a way to define the diagram's visual width. Drag a standard **Note** element from the palette and drop it *inside* the `Diagram` group. Resize this Note to your preferred width. ArchiFabric will use the width of this Note as a placeholder to scale the dynamic diagram in your final PDF.
7. *Important:* Do not drag any actual target views into this template. It must remain abstract so it can be reused.
</documentation>
        <property key="Lead" value="Up to now, we hardcoded our target data (`Deliver Customer Value`) directly into the `Documentation` and `Diagram` artifacts. While this works for single diagrams, it becomes repetitive for larger documents. Instead, we can create a reusable layout template and dynamically pass our target data to it using the **ModelStructure** and **TargetStructure** concepts."/>
      </element>
