        <documentation>To use a Catalog effectively, it is important to understand the distinction between the elements you place inside it:

* **Note elements** are used to define static content and layout formatting.
* **ArchiMate elements** are used to define the *scope and selection*. By placing an ArchiMate element inside a Catalog group, you instruct the engine to filter and extract all elements of that specific type from your target structure.

While you can use any element directly from your model, it is considered best practice to create a dedicated meta-model (for example, aligned with TOGAF standards) in a &quot;Standard Library&quot; folder. Using elements from this meta-model in your templates inherently provides model validation.

For this tutorial, we will focus on extracting **Value Streams**. We will build a template that uses two instances of the *exact same* Value Stream element to display different properties.

**Follow these steps to build the Value Stream catalog:**

1. **Create the Template View:** In your **Tutorial** folder, create a new ArchiMate View and name it `Value Stream Artifact`.
2. **Add the Catalog Artifact:** Drag a **Group** onto the canvas and set its **Name** to `Catalog`.
3. **Add the Filter Element (Header):** Drag a **Value Stream** element from your standard library or model tree into the `Catalog` group. In its Properties panel, set the **Label Expression** to `${header}`. This expression tells ArchiFabric to generate a formatted sub-paragraph header using the name of each found Value Stream.
4. **Add the Filter Element (Documentation):** Drag the *exact same* Value Stream element from the model tree into the `Catalog` group again, placing it below the first one. Set its **Label Expression** to `${documentation}`. This will output the description of the found element.

*Important Tip:* You must use the exact same ArchiMate element from your model tree multiple times within the catalog view to extract different properties of the matched elements.

If you apply this template to a target view, the Catalog artifact will now loop through and display every Value Stream present in that view.

This specific setup is a great milestone. Not only did we introduce the `${header}` and `${documentation}` expressions, but we also laid the groundwork for advanced features in later chapters. In the future, we will use this foundation to explore positional sorting, querying related capabilities per value stream, and repeating elements horizontally to build dynamic tables!</documentation>
        <property key="Lead" value="The `Catalog` artifact works differently from basic layout groups. It acts as a powerful loop, iterating over specific elements from a target and applying a layout to each of them."/>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ArchimateDiagramModel" name="Applying the Value Stream Artifact" id="id-8746a4633a2d4bdeb04e1df8d9f0c826">
        <child xsi:type="archimate:Group" id="id-41f2b3b96462450cb60609231174b960" name="Document" font="1|Noto Sans|9.0|0|GTK|1|" fontColor="#77767b" textAlignment="1" alpha="64">
          <bounds x="420" y="36" width="400" height="313"/>
          <feature name="labelExpression" value="My First Document"/>
