        <documentation>To achieve this, we explicitly model the relationship in our template (the ModelStructure). **ArchiFabric only evaluates relationships that are explicitly drawn and visible on the template canvas.**

We will also use this opportunity to format our nested catalog as a dynamic table, using horizontal positioning and Markdown.

**Follow these steps to add relational data and a table layout:**

1. **Update the Template:** Open your existing `Value Stream Artifact` view.
2. **Add a Nested Catalog:** Drag a new **Group** element *inside* your existing `Catalog sort=position` group, placing it below the Value Stream elements. Set its **Name** to `Catalog`.
3. **Model the Relationship Filter:** Drag a **Capability** element from your standard library (or model tree) into this new nested Catalog. Now, draw a **Serving Relationship** originating from this Capability element and pointing to the Value Stream element above it. This drawn relationship acts as our selection filter: ArchiFabric will now only retrieve Capabilities that actively serve the current Value Stream!
4. **Create Column 1 (Bullet Point):** To build a table-like row, we arrange elements horizontally. Drag a **Note** element into the nested Catalog on the far left. Set its **Label Expression** to `-` (a simple hyphen). The Markdown engine will automatically translate this into a bullet point (unordered list).
5. **Create Column 2 (Bold Name):** Place the Capability element next to the Note. Set its **Label Expression** to `**${name}**`. This uses the Expression Engine to fetch the name, while the surrounding double asterisks (`**`) instruct the Markdown engine to make the text **bold**.
6. **Create Column 3 (Documentation):** Drag the *exact same* Capability element into the nested Catalog and place it on the far right. Set its **Label Expression** to `${documentation}`.
7. **Define Column Proportions:** Resize these three elements horizontally to fit your needs. The physical width of each element on your canvas dictates the relative column width in the generated document! *(Note: The actual styling of the table, such as lines and padding, can be customized later using CSS).*
8. **Generate the Output:** Save the template, go back to your `My First Document` view, and run the ArchiFabric script.</documentation>
        <property key="Lead" value="The true power of ArchiFabric lies in its ability to traverse your architecture model. Instead of just listing elements, you can extract specific elements based on their *relationships* to others. In this section, we will enhance our template to show which Business Capabilities serve each Value Stream."/>
      </element>
    </folder>
  </folder>
  <purpose>**ArchiFabric** is an integrated publishing and documentation suite for Archi, designed to seamlessly weave architecture models, documentation, and publication together into a single cohesive fabric. Traditionally, enterprise architects face a significant disconnect between designing complex models and effectively communicating those insights to business stakeholders. ArchiFabric bridges this gap by transforming the Archi modeling environment into a powerful, automated document generation engine, allowing you to create comprehensive, presentation-ready reports without ever leaving your primary workspace.

At the heart of ArchiFabric lies a highly flexible, modular architecture. Rather than relying on rigid, hardcoded export scripts, it empowers users to design dynamic report templates visually using standard Archi concepts, notes, and relationships. Through an intelligent set of rendering artifacts—such as Catalogs, Matrices, Sections, and Diagrams—architects can visually infer layouts, query specific model scopes, and apply precise filtering mechanisms like Regular Expressions. This data-driven approach ensures that your documentation remains perfectly synchronized with your evolving architecture, granting unparalleled freedom in how information is structured, sorted, and styled.

Furthermore, ArchiFabric is deeply aligned with enterprise architecture best practices, specifically tailored to support the generation of standard TOGAF® artifacts. Whether you need to publish a complex Application Communication Diagram, an automated Business Function to Process Matrix, or a detailed Application Portfolio Catalog, ArchiFabric enables you to produce these deliverables with precision and consistency. By leveraging the visual structure of your models, it turns abstract architectural data into tangible, high-value business communication.

